---
import Pager from "../../components/Pager.astro";
import Picker from "../../components/Picker.astro";
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
---

<Page
  title="Accessibility — oklch-picker"
  description="Built on native range inputs, so keyboard, touch, and screen-reader support come from the platform."
>
  <h1>Accessibility</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Built on native <code>&lt;input type="range"&gt;</code>, so keyboard, touch,
    and screen-reader support come from the platform rather than from
    re-implementations of it.
  </p>

  <h2>What that gives you</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard.</strong> Arrow keys step, <kbd>Home</kbd> and
      <kbd>End</kbd> jump to the ends, <kbd>Page Up</kbd> and
      <kbd>Page Down</kbd> take larger steps — all native behaviour.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Labels.</strong> Each slider carries its own, and the visible
      label is <code>aria-hidden</code> so controls are not announced twice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Touch.</strong> Native range inputs, plus pointer events on the
      draggable chart, so it works the same under a finger as a mouse.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Try it below: tab to a slider and use the arrow keys. Nothing here depends
    on a pointer.
  </p>

  <Picker caption="Tab into the sliders and drive them from the keyboard." />

  <h2>Charts are decoration</h2>

  <p>
    The gamut plots are <code>aria-hidden</code>. They are a visual aid, and the
    sliders reach everything a chart can — including the out-of-gamut regions
    the hatching marks, which the notice also states in words.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the <code>compact</code> layout the visible labels shrink to single
    letters, but the accessible names stay full — a screen reader hears
    "Lightness", not "L".
  </p>

  <Picker layout="compact" caption="Compact: single-letter labels, full accessible names." />

  <h2>Translating it</h2>

  <p>
    The axis names and the out-of-gamut notices come from <code>labels</code>,
    so the picker speaks whatever your app speaks. The keys are the three axes
    — <code>l</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>h</code> — plus
    <code>outOfGamut</code> and a per-space
    <code>outOf:&lt;gamut id&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <Picker
    labels={{
      l: "Helderheid",
      c: "Chroma",
      h: "Tint",
      outOfGamut: "Buiten sRGB — de dichtstbijzijnde sRGB-kleur wordt gebruikt.",
    }}
    caption="The same picker with Dutch labels."
  />

  <Pager />
</Page>
